                        How to purchase a new Ship
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     We are currently offering two different types of ships. The Cog and the
Galleon. The cog is a 2 deck ship with 4 rooms below deck.
The Galleon is a 4 deck ship with 12 rooms below deck.

     There are several different types of rooms available to configure the
lower decks of your ship.  There are 2 weapon rooms, and a cargo room.  It
is neccessary to balance properly your cargo and weapon rooms, so you can
carry the supplies you need, to properly defend yourself.  The two weapon
rooms are Ballistas and Catapults.  They fire different ammunition and are
better at different ranges within combat.  It would be wise to try to have
both on your ship.  If you have a cog, you will be forced to choose to have
one or the other type of weapon, or sacrifice cargo space for your weapons.
Remember, you MUST have cargo rooms, this will decide how far you can sail
before you need to restock on provisions.

     Your first step is to "purchase" one of the ship types.
A cog costs 120,000 gold and includes the minimum crew to run the ship.
A galleon cost 180,000 gold and includes the minimum crew to run the ship.

     Then you must name your fleet, this will be the fleet name that all of
your ships will be under. This is only done when buying your first ship.
Then you will be asked to name this ship. From here you decide on what the
lower decks of the ship will have in them. Your choices are clearly marked
and it starts from the lowest deck to the highest deck below the maindeck.
Then you own a new ship, which we will place in our port for you.


                       How to run a tight ship
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Depending on where you purchase your ship, it can be found in the port
of the town. This is important because this port is where you will need to be
when you recruit sailors and purchase supplies for your ship. First order of
bussiness will be for you to buy supplies and to recruit sailors if you want
to have more then the minimum crew.
     You will buy the needed supplies at the local ship pub. You can also
find sailors to recruit. The ship has a few different items it requires to
be in full operation. Most important it that you always have enough 
provisions onboard for any journey. This is what your crew eat's, and they
won't do anything if they don't get feed.
     Your ship also requires that you purchase wood and rope to repair any
unfortinate damage that you may sustain during a battle. These aren't
required to run the ship, but if you take damage at sea, you will need them
to fix any of the damage.
     You may also want to purchase ammo for your weapon's, if you have any.
You will need to purchase bolts for your ballistas and rocks for your
catapult's. Your ship doesn't come with any of these, so be sure to get some
before leaving port.
     Your crew is also an important part of ship operations. If you fall below
the minimum crew at any time, you won't be able to do anything with your ship
and it will become stranded. To avoid this, we suggest you recruit more 
sailors as soon as possible. The crew has two different skills, they are
rated in sailing and fighting. You also need to have at least the minimum
crew plus 3 for each ballista and 4 for each catapult if you want them to
be ready for battle.

     A small list of orders you can give while onboard ship.

     Captains Quarters
----------------------
The captain can create his own room description by typing "rename description"
where the description is one continous line with a return at the end.
Also, anyone one in the room can emote by typing "em stuff".

     Cargo Hold
----------------------
This is where you will find the ship's "manifest". It will give you a
current list of what is onboard ship. Wood, rope, provisions and ammo.

     Crewroom
----------------------
From here you can find out how the "crew" and "ship" are doing. You can also
order the crew to "repair" the ship when it is damaged. This is also the room
where you can "train" your crew. They start out at the lowest skill level and
progress as you train them.

     Bridge
----------------------
This is where the captain can do the most. The ship has sail's in which you
must order the crew to "furl" or "hoist". To move the ship the sails must be
hoisted. You must "sail DIR" to move the ship. If the ship is in dock, you
must first "push off" before you sail. When your in a port, you must "dock"
for people to enter and leave the ship.
     The ship can also "engage" another, to battle it. Before you engage a
ship you must order the ship to "battlestations", in which orders the crew to
man the ship's weapon's. Once you engage the enemy, you can order the crew to
"fire" your weapons. At anytime during a battle you can try to get into a
advantagous position by trying to "maneuver". If you aren't doing too good or
decide not to attack the other ship, you can try to "disengage". If all is 
sure to be lost, you can "strikecolors", and hope the other captain will
accept your surrender.
     As an admiral of a fleet, you can "accept name" a player outside of your
ship in a port, as a captain of that ship. You can also "deny name" any
captain you previously accepted.
     The ship also flies a flag at all times, you can look at the flag from
here and it is also seen when someone looks at your ship. The flag shows the
current standing of the ship. A green flag is normal ship operations. A blue
flag means the ship is at battlestations. A red flag means the ship is engaged
with another and can be battling. And a white flag means the ship is attempting
to surrender to another.
     From the deck and the bridge, you can "peer" to see the area surrounding
the ship and everything in it. You can also "disembark" from the deck.

While onboard, you can type "orders" in any room to see the list of orders
you may give while in that room.
